      "text": "To find the radius of the circle, we first need to determine the center of the circle. Let's denote the center of the circle by \\((h, 0)\\) since it lies on the \\(x\\)-axis. The distance from the center to any point on the circle is the radius. We will use the distance formula to find the radius.\n\nThe distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\nThe distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to the point \\((1, 3)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\nSince both distances are equal to the radius \\(r\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\nTo eliminate the square roots, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\nExpanding the right side, we get:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n\\]\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(h^2\\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\nSubtracting 10 from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\nDividing both sides by -2, we get:\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\nSo, the center of the circle is at \\((-3, 0)\\). Now, we can find the radius by calculating the distance from the center \\((-3, 0)\\) to the point \\((0, 4)\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(-3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
